/* WISEBYTE LESSONS · Beliefs batch Y: traditions, epics and sacred stories */
WB.L("sikhism", "beliefs", "beginner", "Sikhism: the faith of the free kitchen", "Walk into any gurdwara in the world and someone will offer you a hot meal, no questions asked. Here is the faith behind that open door.",
  [
    ["A meal for anyone", "Langar, the free communal kitchen, is the most visible expression of Sikh values.", "Picture a vast hall at the Golden Temple in Amritsar, India. Rows of people sit cross legged on long mats: farmers, tourists, businesspeople, the very poor. Volunteers move along the lines ladling dal, vegetables and fresh rotis. Nobody pays, nobody is asked their religion or caste, and the kitchen serves tens of thousands of meals on a busy day. This is langar, the free community kitchen found at every gurdwara, or Sikh place of worship. Sitting together on the floor is deliberate: it puts everyone at the same level. For Sikhs, langar is not charity handed down from above but a practical way of living out equality. If you want to understand Sikhism, the kitchen is a very good place to start."],
    ["Guru Nanak's vision", "Sikhism began with Guru Nanak in Punjab in the late 1400s.", "Sikhism was founded in the Punjab region of South Asia by Guru Nanak, who was born in 1469. Sikh tradition tells how, as a young man, he disappeared into a river for three days and returned with a striking message: 'There is no Hindu, there is no Muslim.' Sikhs understand this as a call to look past religious labels to a shared humanity and a single God. Nanak spent years travelling and teaching through poetry and song, then settled in a village where his followers farmed and worshipped together. His teachings rejected empty ritual and the caste system, and stressed honest work, sharing with others and remembering God. The word Sikh itself means learner or disciple, which captures the spirit of the tradition well."],
    ["One Creator", "Sikhs believe in one formless God, expressed in the phrase Ik Onkar.", "The first words of the Sikh scripture are Ik Onkar, usually translated as 'One Creator' or 'There is one God'. The symbol for it appears on gurdwara walls, jewellery and car stickers around the world. Sikhs believe God has no physical form, gender or birth, and is present in all of creation. The goal of life is to move from self centredness, called haumai or ego, towards awareness of God. Sikh teaching describes three daily practices that help with this: Naam Japna, remembering God through meditation and prayer; Kirat Karni, earning an honest living; and Vand Chakna, sharing what you have with others. These are simple to state and demanding to live, which is rather the point."],
    ["The ten Gurus and a living book", "After ten human Gurus, the scripture itself became the Guru.", "Guru Nanak was followed by nine more human Gurus, each building on his teachings over roughly two centuries. The fifth Guru, Arjan, compiled the hymns of the Gurus and of some Hindu and Muslim saints into a single scripture. The tenth Guru, Gobind Singh, made a remarkable decision before his death in 1708: there would be no more human Gurus. Instead, the scripture, the Guru Granth Sahib, would be the eternal Guru. It is treated with deep respect. In a gurdwara it rests on a raised platform under a canopy, someone waves a whisk over it, and at night it is carried ceremonially to its own resting room. Visitors cover their heads and remove their shoes before entering its presence."],
    ["The Khalsa and the Five Ks", "Initiated Sikhs keep five articles of faith, including uncut hair.", "In 1699, at the spring festival of Vaisakhi, Guru Gobind Singh created the Khalsa, a community of initiated Sikhs committed to discipline, courage and defending the vulnerable. Members keep five articles of faith, known as the Five Ks because each begins with that sound in Punjabi. Kesh is uncut hair, often covered by a turban. Kara is a steel bracelet. Kanga is a small wooden comb. Kachera is a cotton undergarment. Kirpan is a small ceremonial blade, a symbol of standing against injustice. Many Sikh men take the surname Singh, meaning lion, and many Sikh women take Kaur, often translated as princess. Using shared surnames was another way of rejecting caste, since surnames in the region often revealed it."],
    ["The turban", "For many Sikhs, the turban is a commitment rather than a fashion choice.", "The turban, or dastaar, is the most recognisable sign of a Sikh. It covers and protects uncut hair, and many Sikhs describe tying it each morning as an act of identity and faith. It also carries a message: historically, turbans were associated with nobility, and the Gurus encouraged all Sikhs to carry themselves with dignity regardless of birth. Some Sikh women also wear turbans, while others cover their hair with a scarf. Because the turban is so visible, Sikhs have sometimes been mistaken for members of other groups and faced harassment, especially after the September 2001 attacks in the United States. Many Sikh organisations now run awareness days where people can try on a turban and ask questions."],
    ["Sikhs in Australia", "Sikhs have lived in Australia since the 1800s and are now a fast growing community.", "Sikhs are not newcomers to Australia. In the 1800s, Punjabi men worked here as hawkers, cameleers and farm labourers, travelling the bush selling goods from carts. The coastal town of Woolgoolga in northern New South Wales became home to a Punjabi farming community, and one of Australia's earliest gurdwaras was built there in the late 1960s. Today Sikhism is one of the fastest growing religions in the country, with more than 200,000 people identifying as Sikh in the 2021 census. Worldwide there are around 25 to 30 million Sikhs, most of them in Punjab. Many Australian gurdwaras are known for cooking langar for communities hit by bushfires and floods, carrying the free kitchen tradition into new places."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What is langar?", ["A Sikh prayer said at dawn", "A free communal kitchen at every gurdwara", "The steel bracelet worn by Sikhs", "A Punjabi harvest festival"], 1, "Langar is the free kitchen where anyone, of any background, is served a meal while seated as equals."],
    [1, "Who founded Sikhism?", ["Guru Gobind Singh", "Guru Arjan", "Guru Nanak", "Ranjit Singh"], 2, "Guru Nanak, born in Punjab in 1469, is the founder of Sikhism."],
    [3, "What did Guru Gobind Singh declare would be the eternal Guru?", ["The Golden Temple", "The Khalsa council", "His eldest son", "The scripture, the Guru Granth Sahib"], 3, "Before his death in 1708 he declared the scripture itself would be the Guru, ending the line of human Gurus."],
    [4, "Which of these is one of the Five Ks?", ["Kara, a steel bracelet", "Kurta, a long shirt", "Kalash, a sacred pot", "Kirtan, devotional singing"], 0, "The kara, a steel bracelet, is one of the five articles of faith alongside kesh, kanga, kachera and kirpan."],
    [6, "When did Sikhs first come to Australia?", ["Only after the Second World War", "In the 1800s, working as hawkers and cameleers", "In the 1990s as students", "With the First Fleet in 1788"], 1, "Punjabi Sikhs worked in colonial Australia in the 1800s as hawkers, cameleers and labourers."]
  ]);

WB.L("shinto", "beliefs", "beginner", "Shinto: Japan's way of the kami", "Japan is dotted with more than 80,000 shrines, marked by bright red gates. Behind them sits a tradition with no founder, no single holy book and a world full of spirits.",
  [
    ["Through the red gate", "Shinto is Japan's native tradition of honouring the kami.", "Stand at the edge of a Japanese forest and you may see it: a tall gate of two posts and two crossbeams, often painted vermilion. This is a torii, and it marks the boundary between the everyday world and sacred ground. Beyond it lies a Shinto shrine, home to a kami. Shinto, often translated as 'the way of the kami', is Japan's oldest religious tradition. Unlike many religions it has no founder, no single sacred scripture and no fixed list of beliefs that followers must sign up to. It is woven instead through festivals, rituals and a deep sense that the natural world is alive with sacred presence. Japan has tens of thousands of shrines, from tiny roadside boxes to grand complexes."],
    ["What is a kami?", "Kami are sacred powers found in nature, places, ancestors and great people.", "Kami is hard to translate. It can mean a god, a spirit or simply something that inspires awe. A kami might live in a mountain like Fuji, an ancient tree, a waterfall or an oddly shaped rock, which is why you sometimes see a rope with paper streamers tied around a boulder or trunk. Some kami are great figures of mythology; others are the spirits of ancestors or of notable people honoured after death. Traditionally people speak of 'eight million kami', a phrase meaning countless rather than an exact number. Kami are not thought of as all powerful or perfectly good. They can be gentle or rough, and much of Shinto practice is about keeping a respectful, harmonious relationship with them."],
    ["Born from a spear", "Japan's ancient chronicles tell how two deities created the islands of Japan.", "Japan's oldest surviving books, the Kojiki from 712 and the Nihon Shoki from 720, record its myths. In them, two deities, Izanagi and Izanami, stand on the floating bridge of heaven and stir the ocean with a jewelled spear. Brine dripping from its tip forms the first island, and the pair go on to give birth to the islands of Japan and many kami. When Izanami dies giving birth to the fire god, the grieving Izanagi follows her to the land of the dead, much as Orpheus does in Greek myth, and flees in horror when he sees her decay. Washing himself clean afterwards, he gives rise to new kami, including the sun goddess Amaterasu, from his left eye."],
    ["The sun goddess in the cave", "The myth of Amaterasu shows how laughter and ritual brought light back to the world.", "One of the best loved Shinto stories tells of Amaterasu, the sun goddess. Upset by the destructive pranks of her storm god brother Susanoo, she shut herself in a cave and the world fell into darkness. The other kami gathered outside and tried to lure her out. They hung a mirror and jewels on a tree, and the goddess Ame no Uzume performed a wild, comic dance on an upturned tub that made all the kami roar with laughter. Curious about the noise, Amaterasu peeked out, glimpsed her own radiance in the mirror and was drawn into the open. Light returned. The mirror, a sword and a jewel later became the imperial regalia associated with Japan's emperors."],
    ["Purity and the shrine visit", "Shinto places great importance on purification before meeting the kami.", "A central idea in Shinto is purity. Impurity, called kegare, is not quite the same as sin. It is more like spiritual dirt that comes from contact with death, illness or wrongdoing, and it can be washed away. At the entrance to most shrines is a stone basin with wooden ladles. Visitors rinse their left hand, then their right, then their mouth, before approaching the main hall. There they often toss a coin into a box, bow twice, clap twice, make a wish or prayer, and bow once more. Priests perform larger purification rituals, waving a wand of paper streamers. Visitors can buy omamori charms for luck or safe driving, and omikuji, paper fortunes tied to racks if the news is bad."],
    ["Rebuilding forever", "Japan's most sacred shrine, at Ise, is rebuilt every twenty years.", "The Grand Shrine of Ise, dedicated to Amaterasu, is the most sacred site in Shinto. Its main buildings are made of plain cypress wood with thatched roofs, and every twenty years they are carefully rebuilt on a neighbouring plot, then the old ones are taken down. The most recent rebuild was in 2013. This tradition has continued, with some interruptions, for well over a thousand years. It keeps carpentry skills alive across generations and expresses a Shinto idea that renewal and freshness are sacred. It also raises a lovely puzzle: is a building that is constantly rebuilt very new or very old? Western ideas of preserving original materials do not quite fit, and visitors often find that question as memorable as the shrine itself."],
    ["Shinto in modern Japan", "Most Japanese people mix Shinto and Buddhist practices without seeing a conflict.", "Ask Japanese people whether they are religious and many will say no, yet most take part in Shinto customs. Millions visit shrines in the first days of the New Year, babies are often presented at a shrine, and weddings may follow Shinto rites. Funerals, however, are usually Buddhist. For centuries Shinto and Buddhism were blended, until the government separated them in the 1860s. From then until 1945, a nationalist form called State Shinto promoted the emperor as divine. After the Second World War, Emperor Hirohito publicly rejected the idea that he was a living god, and Japan's new constitution separated religion and the state. Today Shinto is mostly about community festivals, seasonal rituals and respect for nature."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What does a torii gate mark?", ["The entrance to a Buddhist monastery", "The boundary into sacred ground at a Shinto shrine", "A royal palace", "A graveyard"], 1, "A torii marks the passage from the everyday world into the sacred space of a shrine."],
    [1, "What does the phrase 'eight million kami' suggest?", ["An exact census of spirits", "The number of shrines in Japan", "That kami are countless", "The number of emperors"], 2, "The phrase is a way of saying there are countless kami, not a precise number."],
    [3, "How was Amaterasu drawn out of her cave?", ["Her brother apologised", "The other kami laughed at a comic dance and she peeked out to see why", "A great earthquake shook the cave open", "The emperor ordered her to leave"], 1, "The laughter at Ame no Uzume's dance made Amaterasu curious, and she was lured out by her own reflection."],
    [4, "What do shrine visitors usually do at the stone water basin?", ["Drink deeply for good luck", "Throw coins for wishes", "Rinse their hands and mouth to purify themselves", "Wash their feet"], 2, "Rinsing the hands and mouth is a purification ritual before approaching the kami."],
    [5, "What is unusual about the Grand Shrine of Ise?", ["It is made entirely of stone", "It is underwater at high tide", "It is rebuilt every twenty years", "Only the emperor may see it"], 2, "Ise's main buildings are rebuilt on a neighbouring site every twenty years, most recently in 2013."]
  ]);

WB.L("aboriginal-dreaming", "beliefs", "intermediate", "The Dreaming: law, land and story", "For tens of thousands of years, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples have carried knowledge in story, song and country. 'Dreamtime' barely begins to describe it.",
  [
    ["More than a story time", "The Dreaming is a living system of law, belief and knowledge, not a distant past.", "Many Australians first meet Aboriginal spirituality through the word 'Dreamtime', often imagined as a magical era long ago. That picture misses a great deal. What is commonly called the Dreaming is a whole framework that links creation, law, kinship, land and daily responsibility. It explains how the world came to be, but it also tells people how to live, whom they may marry, how to care for country and what knowledge belongs to whom. Australia's First Peoples have lived here for at least 50,000 years, and their cultures are among the oldest continuous cultures on Earth. This lesson describes the Dreaming in general terms, because there is no single version: each nation has its own beliefs and its own words."],
    ["Where the word came from", "'Dreaming' is an English translation, and many communities use their own terms.", "The English words 'Dreamtime' and 'Dreaming' were popularised by anthropologists in the late 1800s, translating an Arrernte word from Central Australia that is often written altyerre. Many Aboriginal people and scholars feel the translation is clumsy, because it suggests something imaginary or unreal, when these beliefs describe what is most real. Other communities have their own terms. The Anangu people of Uluru speak of Tjukurpa, and the Warlpiri of the Tanami Desert speak of Jukurrpa. In the 1950s the anthropologist W. E. H. Stanner described the Dreaming as an 'everywhen', a phrase that is still widely quoted. He meant that ancestral events are not only in the past: they continue to shape the present and the future."],
    ["Ancestral beings and country", "Ancestral beings shaped the land, and their presence remains in it.", "In many Dreaming accounts, ancestral beings travelled across a formless or sleeping land, and their actions created its features. A waterhole may mark where an ancestor rested, a ridge where one lay down, a rock formation where a battle took place. These beings took many forms: animals, plants, people, stars, and powerful figures like the Rainbow Serpent, which appears in different ways in stories across much of the continent and is often associated with water and fertility. Because ancestors are believed to remain present in the land, country is not just scenery or property. It is family, history and law all at once. Many Aboriginal people speak of belonging to country, rather than country belonging to them."],
    ["Songlines", "Songlines are routes across the land that carry stories, law and practical knowledge.", "The journeys of ancestral beings are often remembered as songlines, sometimes called Dreaming tracks. A songline can stretch hundreds or even thousands of kilometres, crossing the lands of many nations, with each group holding responsibility for its own section of the song. Singing the verses in order works like a map: it names waterholes, landmarks and food sources in sequence, which could help people travel safely through dry country. Songlines also carry law, kinship rules and seasonal knowledge, and some follow patterns in the stars. Researchers studying memory have taken a keen interest in how songs, places and stories together can hold huge amounts of information without writing. For the custodians, though, songlines are first and foremost sacred."],
    ["Not every story is for everyone", "Some Dreaming knowledge is restricted, and sharing it needs the right authority.", "A key point for outsiders is that Dreaming knowledge is not public property. Some stories are open, often told to children and visitors. Others are restricted to people who have gone through certain ceremonies, or are known as men's business or women's business and kept within those groups. Certain sites are sacred and should not be photographed or entered. Rights to tell a story, paint its designs or sing its songs belong to particular custodians, who inherit that responsibility through kinship. This is why respectful books, galleries and tourism operators work with traditional owners and seek permission. It is also why this lesson does not retell specific sacred stories: the respectful way to hear them is from the people who hold them."],
    ["Uluru and Tjukurpa", "At Uluru, Anangu law guided the 2019 decision to close the climb.", "Uluru in the Northern Territory shows how the Dreaming shapes modern life. For the Anangu traditional owners, Uluru is a place where Tjukurpa is present, and the route visitors once climbed has deep spiritual significance. For decades, signs at the base politely asked tourists not to climb, and many did anyway. After the park was handed back to Anangu ownership in 1985 and leased to the national parks agency, the joint management board eventually voted to end the climb, and it closed permanently in October 2019. Today visitors walk around the base instead, where guides share stories that are open to the public. Rangers explain which areas are sensitive and why some features should not be photographed."],
    ["A living tradition", "The Dreaming continues today through ceremony, art, language and care for country.", "It is a mistake to describe the Dreaming in the past tense. Colonisation caused enormous harm: dispossession, the loss of many languages and the removal of children from families broke many lines of transmission. Yet ceremony, song and story have survived, and many communities are working hard to revive languages and pass knowledge on. Art has become one of the most visible bridges. Since the 1970s, painters from places like Papunya in the Western Desert have used acrylic paint to share permitted aspects of their Dreamings, creating a world famous art movement. Ranger programs blend traditional knowledge with modern land management. When you hear a Welcome to Country, you are hearing a small part of a very old relationship with land."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Which description of the Dreaming is most accurate?", ["A single shared story told the same way across Australia", "A system linking creation, law, kinship, land and responsibility", "A children's bedtime tradition", "A belief that ended with colonisation"], 1, "The Dreaming is a whole framework of law, belief and knowledge, and it differs between nations."],
    [1, "Who described the Dreaming as an 'everywhen'?", ["Baldwin Spencer", "Albert Namatjira", "W. E. H. Stanner", "Captain James Cook"], 2, "The anthropologist W. E. H. Stanner used the term in the 1950s to convey that ancestral events continue into the present."],
    [3, "What is one practical function of a songline?", ["It records rainfall statistics", "It names landmarks and waterholes in order, like a map", "It marks colonial property boundaries", "It sets the dates of school holidays"], 1, "Sung in order, a songline names places and resources along a route, helping people navigate country."],
    [4, "Why might a respectful book avoid retelling certain Dreaming stories?", ["They are too short", "They have all been lost", "Some knowledge is restricted and belongs to particular custodians", "They are only told in English"], 2, "Some stories are restricted, and the right to share them belongs to specific custodians."],
    [5, "When did the climb at Uluru permanently close?", ["October 2019", "January 1985", "March 2001", "It is still open"], 0, "The climb closed permanently in October 2019, following a decision by the park's board, which has an Anangu majority."]
  ]);

WB.L("epic-of-gilgamesh", "beliefs", "intermediate", "Gilgamesh: the world's oldest great story", "A tyrant king, a wild man from the steppe and a desperate quest to cheat death. Written on clay over 3,000 years ago, Gilgamesh still feels startlingly modern.",
  [
    ["Clay that kept a secret", "Gilgamesh was rediscovered in the 1800s on clay tablets from ancient Nineveh.", "In 1872 a young assistant at the British Museum named George Smith was piecing together broken clay tablets dug up in the ruins of Nineveh, in modern Iraq. Reading the wedge shaped cuneiform script, he found a story of a great flood, a man who built a boat, and a bird sent out to look for dry land. It looked strikingly like the story of Noah. According to a well known account, Smith was so excited he leapt up and began rushing around the room. The tablets came from the library of the Assyrian king Ashurbanipal, from the 600s BCE. They held the Epic of Gilgamesh, a poem whose roots go back far earlier, and it caused a sensation."],
    ["A king who might have been real", "Gilgamesh may have been a real king of Uruk, later turned into a legend.", "The story is set in Uruk, one of the first great cities, in southern Mesopotamia. Ancient king lists name Gilgamesh as one of its rulers, and many historians think a real king of that name may have reigned around 2700 BCE, though there is no direct proof. Over the centuries he grew into a figure of legend, described as two thirds god and one third human. Poems about him were first written in Sumerian, probably around 2100 BCE, and later woven into a longer Babylonian epic. The best known version, spread over twelve tablets, is traditionally credited to a scholar named Sin leqi unninni. In other words, Gilgamesh is part history, part myth and part great literature."],
    ["The tyrant and the wild man", "The gods create Enkidu to balance Gilgamesh, and the two become close friends.", "The epic opens with a problem. Gilgamesh is magnificent but arrogant, and he oppresses the people of Uruk, who cry out to the gods. The gods respond by creating Enkidu, a wild man who lives among the animals of the steppe, eating grass and drinking at waterholes. A woman named Shamhat is sent to bring him into human society, and he learns to eat bread, drink beer and wear clothes. When Enkidu finally confronts Gilgamesh, the two wrestle fiercely in the street. Neither can fully defeat the other, and their struggle turns into a deep friendship. Many readers see in this a story about civilisation and nature, and about how friendship can tame pride."],
    ["Monsters and an angry goddess", "Gilgamesh and Enkidu kill the guardian of the Cedar Forest and the Bull of Heaven.", "Seeking lasting fame, Gilgamesh persuades Enkidu to travel to the distant Cedar Forest and kill its fearsome guardian, Humbaba. With help from the sun god Shamash, they succeed and cut down the great cedars. Back in Uruk, the goddess Ishtar is so impressed by Gilgamesh that she proposes marriage. He rejects her rudely, listing the sad fates of her previous lovers. Furious, she sends the Bull of Heaven to ravage the land, and the two heroes slay it too. Enkidu even throws a piece of the bull at the goddess. It is thrilling adventure, but the gods take note. They decide that one of the friends must die for these offences, and the lot falls on Enkidu."],
    ["Grief and the fear of death", "Enkidu's death turns the epic into a meditation on mortality.", "Enkidu falls ill and dies after days of suffering, and Gilgamesh is shattered. He refuses to let his friend be buried until, in a famously grim detail, a worm falls from Enkidu's nose. The mighty king suddenly understands that he too will die. This is the emotional heart of the epic, and it is what makes Gilgamesh feel so human more than 3,000 years later. He tears off his fine clothes, dresses in animal skins and wanders the wilderness, consumed by grief and terror. His question is one every reader recognises: is there any way to escape death? He sets out to find the one man who is said to have been granted eternal life."],
    ["The flood survivor", "Utnapishtim tells Gilgamesh how he survived a great flood sent by the gods.", "After a long journey, including crossing the waters of death, Gilgamesh reaches Utnapishtim, a wise man who lives with his wife at the edge of the world. Utnapishtim tells his story. The gods decided to destroy humanity with a flood, but the god Ea secretly warned him to build a huge boat and load it with his family, craftsmen and animals. The storm raged for days. When it ended, the boat came to rest on a mountain, and Utnapishtim released a dove, a swallow and then a raven, which did not return because it had found land. Grateful, he made an offering, and the gods granted him and his wife immortality. Scholars still discuss how this story relates to the biblical flood."],
    ["The plant, the snake and the wall", "Gilgamesh loses his chance at youth, and returns home to find a different kind of immortality.", "Utnapishtim tests Gilgamesh by challenging him to stay awake for a week, and the exhausted king immediately falls asleep. Out of kindness, Utnapishtim tells him of a plant at the bottom of the sea that restores youth. Gilgamesh ties stones to his feet, dives down and gets it. But on the way home, while he bathes in a pool, a snake slithers up and eats the plant, shedding its skin as it goes. Gilgamesh weeps. He returns to Uruk empty handed, and the epic ends with him showing a companion the city's mighty walls and brickwork. The message many readers take is that we cannot live forever, but our work, our cities and our stories can outlast us."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What did George Smith discover in 1872?", ["The ruins of Uruk", "A flood story on tablets from Nineveh", "The tomb of Gilgamesh", "The Rosetta Stone"], 1, "Smith found a flood story in the Gilgamesh tablets from Ashurbanipal's library at Nineveh."],
    [2, "How do Gilgamesh and Enkidu become friends?", ["They are raised as brothers", "They fight the same army", "They wrestle, neither fully wins, and they bond", "Ishtar introduces them"], 2, "Their fierce wrestling match ends in mutual respect and a deep friendship."],
    [3, "Why do the gods decide one hero must die?", ["For killing Humbaba and the Bull of Heaven", "For refusing to pay taxes", "For leaving Uruk without permission", "For stealing the plant of youth"], 0, "Killing the forest guardian and the Bull of Heaven offended the gods, and Enkidu is chosen to die."],
    [5, "Which bird did not return to Utnapishtim's boat?", ["The dove", "The swallow", "The eagle", "The raven"], 3, "The raven did not come back, showing that it had found dry land."],
    [6, "What happens to the plant that restores youth?", ["Gilgamesh eats it and lives forever", "A snake eats it while Gilgamesh bathes", "Enkidu's ghost takes it", "Ishtar destroys it"], 1, "A snake eats the plant and sheds its skin, and Gilgamesh loses his chance at renewed youth."]
  ]);

WB.L("flood-myths", "beliefs", "advanced", "The great flood stories", "Noah's ark is famous, but it is one of many. From Mesopotamia to India, Greece and China, cultures have told of a world drowned and begun again.",
  [
    ["A story told many times", "Flood stories appear in many cultures, each with its own meaning.", "Imagine rain that does not stop, rivers that swallow fields, then a single boat afloat on an endless sea. Versions of this image appear in sacred stories around the world. In the Hebrew Bible, Noah builds an ark for his family and the animals. In ancient Mesopotamia, several heroes survive a flood sent by the gods. In Hindu tradition, a fish warns the first man of a coming deluge. Greek, Chinese and many Indigenous traditions have their own versions. For believers, some of these accounts carry deep religious meaning. For historians, the pattern raises interesting questions about shared memories, borrowed stories and the experience of living beside water. This lesson looks at both, without assuming any single answer."],
    ["Mesopotamia's floods", "The oldest written flood stories come from ancient Mesopotamia.", "The earliest known written flood stories come from Mesopotamia, the land between the Tigris and Euphrates rivers in modern Iraq. A Sumerian account tells of a king named Ziusudra who survives a flood. A Babylonian poem, the Atrahasis epic, explains that the gods sent the flood because humans had become too numerous and noisy, disturbing their rest. The wise Atrahasis is secretly warned by the god Enki and builds a boat. The same story appears in the Epic of Gilgamesh, where the survivor is called Utnapishtim. These tablets date back to roughly the early second millennium BCE. In a region where great rivers regularly burst their banks and flat land stretched for kilometres, a catastrophic flood was a very real fear."],
    ["Noah and the covenant", "In Genesis, the flood is about human wrongdoing, judgment and a new promise.", "The story of Noah appears in the Book of Genesis and is shared by Judaism and Christianity, with a version in the Quran, where Noah is known as Nuh. In Genesis, God sees that the earth is filled with violence and decides to send a flood, but chooses the righteous Noah to survive with his family and pairs of animals. After the waters recede, Noah sends out a raven and a dove, and the dove returns with an olive leaf. God then makes a covenant, or solemn promise, never again to destroy the earth by flood, with the rainbow as its sign. For many believers, the story is about faithfulness, justice and mercy, whatever view they take on its historical detail."],
    ["Similar but different", "Scholars note close parallels between Genesis and Mesopotamian accounts, alongside big differences.", "When George Smith translated the Gilgamesh flood tablet in 1872, readers were struck by the parallels with Noah: a warning from the divine, a great boat, animals aboard, a landing on a mountain, birds sent out to find land and a sacrifice afterwards. Many scholars think the Genesis account and the Mesopotamian stories draw on a shared regional tradition, since the Israelites lived in the same wider cultural world. But the differences matter just as much. In the Mesopotamian versions, many quarrelsome gods send the flood for reasons that can seem petty. In Genesis, one God acts in response to moral wrongdoing and ends with a promise. Same shape, very different message, which is often how traditions reshape shared stories."],
    ["Fish, stones and dams", "Hindu, Greek and Chinese flood stories each have their own twist.", "In Hindu tradition, the first man, Manu, finds a tiny fish that asks for protection. He cares for it as it grows huge, and it turns out to be the god Vishnu in his fish form, Matsya. The fish warns of a flood and tows Manu's boat to safety. In Greek myth, Deucalion and his wife Pyrrha survive a flood sent by Zeus, then repopulate the world by throwing stones over their shoulders, which become people. Chinese tradition tells of Yu the Great, who succeeded where his father had failed. Instead of building dams to block the water, Yu dug channels to guide it to the sea. The Chinese story is less about divine punishment and more about wise human effort."],
    ["Memories of rising seas", "Some flood stories may preserve real memories of changing coastlines.", "Could some flood stories remember real events? At the end of the last Ice Age, melting ice sheets raised global sea levels by well over 100 metres, a process that largely ended around 7,000 years ago. Coastlines around the world moved inland. In Australia, researchers including linguist Nicholas Reid and geographer Patrick Nunn have studied Aboriginal stories from many coastal areas that describe the sea rising and land being lost, and argue that some may preserve memories from that time. Other researchers have proposed that a sudden flooding of the Black Sea inspired ancient legends, though that idea remains debated. These theories are interesting but not settled, and none of them proves or disproves any particular religious account."],
    ["Why floods make good stories", "Flood stories speak to fear, renewal and the hope of a fresh start.", "There are practical reasons flood stories are so widespread. Most early civilisations grew along rivers or coasts, where floods were both a threat and a source of fertile soil, so disaster was a shared experience worth remembering. But the stories endure for deeper reasons too. A flood wipes the slate clean, so it lets a tradition ask big questions. Why do bad things happen? What do we owe each other and the divine? Who deserves to survive, and why? The survivors usually start a new world, so the stories are as much about hope as about destruction. In a time of climate change and rising seas, these ancient tales can feel surprisingly relevant to people of every belief."]
  ],
  [
    [1, "According to the Atrahasis epic, why did the gods send a flood?", ["Humans had stopped making sacrifices", "Humans had become too numerous and noisy", "A king had insulted the sun god", "The rivers needed to be refilled"], 1, "In Atrahasis, the gods are disturbed by the noise of an overpopulated humanity."],
    [2, "In Genesis, what is the sign of God's covenant after the flood?", ["A dove", "An olive tree", "A rainbow", "A mountain"], 2, "The rainbow is given as the sign of the promise never again to destroy the earth by flood."],
    [3, "What is a key difference between Genesis and the Mesopotamian flood accounts?", ["Only Genesis includes a boat", "Only Mesopotamian accounts include animals", "In Genesis one God acts in response to moral wrongdoing", "Genesis has no birds"], 2, "Mesopotamian versions feature many gods with sometimes petty motives, while Genesis centres on one God, wrongdoing and a promise."],
    [4, "How did Yu the Great tame the flood in Chinese tradition?", ["By building taller dams", "By praying to the dragon king", "By digging channels to guide water to the sea", "By building an ark"], 2, "Yu succeeded by channelling the water, where his father's dams had failed."],
    [5, "What real event might some Aboriginal coastal stories remember, according to some researchers?", ["A tsunami in 1900", "Sea level rise after the last Ice Age", "The flooding of the Murray in 1956", "A comet strike"], 1, "Researchers have argued some stories may preserve memories of coastlines lost as seas rose after the last Ice Age."]
  ]);

WB.L("zoroastrianism", "beliefs", "advanced", "Zoroastrianism: the fire and the choice", "Once the faith of Persian empires, now held by a small community, Zoroastrianism asks every person to pick a side in a cosmic battle between good and evil.",
  [
    ["The flame that never goes out", "Zoroastrian fire temples keep sacred fires burning as symbols of purity.", "In the Iranian city of Yazd, inside a fire temple, a flame burns in a large metal vessel behind glass. According to the community, it has been kept alight for many centuries, tended carefully by priests who add sandalwood and recite prayers. Outsiders sometimes call Zoroastrians 'fire worshippers', but that is a misunderstanding. For Zoroastrians, fire is a powerful symbol of purity, light and the presence of the divine, and prayers are said in the presence of fire rather than to it. Zoroastrianism is one of the world's oldest living religions. It shaped the beliefs of ancient Persian empires and, many scholars think, influenced other faiths. Today its followers number only in the low hundreds of thousands worldwide."],
    ["The prophet Zarathustra", "Zoroastrianism traces back to the prophet Zarathustra, whose dates are uncertain.", "The religion takes its name from its prophet, Zarathustra, known to the Greeks as Zoroaster. Very little is certain about his life. Scholars disagree about when he lived, with estimates ranging from around 1500 BCE to about 600 BCE, and many favour an early date based on the language of his hymns. Those hymns, called the Gathas, are written in an ancient language known as Avestan and are the oldest part of the Zoroastrian scripture, the Avesta. In them, Zarathustra speaks directly and personally to God, asking questions about justice, truth and how a person should live. Tradition says he was a priest who received a vision, then faced strong opposition before winning the support of a local king."],
    ["Wise Lord and the destructive spirit", "Zoroastrians worship Ahura Mazda, the Wise Lord, who is opposed by a destructive spirit.", "Zoroastrians worship one supreme God, Ahura Mazda, whose name means Wise Lord. Ahura Mazda is entirely good and is the creator of all that is good. Opposed to him is Angra Mainyu, the destructive or hostile spirit, the source of evil, sickness and death. The world is the scene of a struggle between asha, meaning truth and right order, and druj, meaning the lie or disorder. Scholars debate whether this makes the faith dualistic, with two opposing powers, or monotheistic with a powerful adversary, and Zoroastrians themselves generally describe their faith as monotheistic. Either way, the key idea is that evil is not part of God's nature. It is something that will ultimately be defeated."],
    ["Good thoughts, good words, good deeds", "Every person has free will and must choose to side with good.", "At the heart of Zoroastrian ethics is a simple three part motto: good thoughts, good words, good deeds. Because the world is a battleground, every human choice counts. People are not born sinful; they have free will and can strengthen the side of good through honesty, kindness, hard work and care for the natural world. Zoroastrians also place great value on purity, including keeping earth, water and fire clean. Young people are initiated in a ceremony called the navjote, where they receive a white undershirt, the sudreh, and a sacred cord, the kusti, which is tied around the waist. Many Zoroastrians retie the kusti several times a day while saying prayers, a regular reminder to choose good."],
    ["The bridge of judgment", "Zoroastrian teaching describes judgment after death and a final renewal of the world.", "Zoroastrianism has a vivid picture of what happens after death. Tradition teaches that on the third night after a person dies, the soul crosses the Chinvat Bridge. For the righteous, the bridge is wide and they pass to the House of Song, a paradise. For the wicked it narrows like a blade, and they fall into a place of suffering. At the end of time, a saviour figure called the Saoshyant will appear, the dead will be raised, evil will be defeated and the world will be renewed in perfection. Many scholars think these ideas of individual judgment, heaven and hell, resurrection and a final saviour influenced Judaism, Christianity and Islam, though the extent of that influence is still debated."],
    ["Faith of empires", "Zoroastrianism was closely tied to the great Persian empires.", "Zoroastrian beliefs were bound up with Persian power for over a thousand years. Inscriptions of the Achaemenid king Darius the Great, who ruled from about 522 BCE, credit his rule to Ahuramazda, a form of Ahura Mazda. The Sasanian empire, from the 200s CE, made Zoroastrianism its official religion, with a powerful priesthood and grand fire temples. Many people believe the Magi who visit the infant Jesus in the Gospel of Matthew were Zoroastrian priests, as magi was a term for that priestly class, though the Gospel does not say so directly. After the Arab conquest of Persia in the 600s CE, Islam gradually became the majority faith, and Zoroastrians became a shrinking minority in their homeland."],
    ["The Parsis and a small community today", "Many Zoroastrians today are Parsis, descendants of migrants to India.", "According to tradition, a group of Zoroastrians left Persia and sailed to the coast of Gujarat in India, perhaps around the 700s to 900s CE, seeking freedom to practise their faith. Their descendants are called Parsis, meaning Persians. Many later settled in Mumbai, where Parsis became prominent in business, law and philanthropy; the Tata family is a famous example. The rock star Freddie Mercury, born Farrokh Bulsara, came from a Parsi family. Traditionally Parsis placed the dead in Towers of Silence to be exposed to vultures, to avoid polluting earth or fire, though this is changing. The community is small and ageing, and debates about intermarriage and conversion are lively. Iran's festival of Nowruz has Zoroastrian roots."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "How do Zoroastrians understand the sacred fire?", ["As a god to be worshipped", "As a symbol of purity and the divine, in whose presence they pray", "As a weapon against demons", "As a way to cook offerings"], 1, "Fire is a symbol of purity and divine presence; prayers are said before it, not to it."],
    [2, "What does the name Ahura Mazda mean?", ["Destructive Spirit", "Eternal Flame", "Wise Lord", "King of Kings"], 2, "Ahura Mazda means Wise Lord, the supreme and wholly good God."],
    [3, "What is the Zoroastrian three part motto?", ["Pray, fast, give", "Good thoughts, good words, good deeds", "Faith, hope, charity", "Truth, beauty, wisdom"], 1, "Good thoughts, good words and good deeds sum up Zoroastrian ethics."],
    [4, "What happens at the Chinvat Bridge, according to tradition?", ["Kings are crowned", "Souls are judged after death", "The sacred fire is lit", "The navjote ceremony takes place"], 1, "The soul crosses the Chinvat Bridge after death, and it widens for the righteous and narrows for the wicked."],
    [6, "Who are the Parsis?", ["Zoroastrian priests in Iran", "Descendants of Zoroastrians who migrated to India", "An ancient Persian dynasty", "Followers of a breakaway Islamic sect"], 1, "Parsis descend from Zoroastrians who, by tradition, sailed from Persia to Gujarat in India."]
  ]);

WB.L("daoism", "beliefs", "beginner", "Daoism: the way of water", "A short ancient book, a sage who may never have existed and the idea that doing less can achieve more. Daoism has shaped Chinese life for over 2,000 years.",
  [
    ["The softest thing in the world", "Daoism often uses water as an image of quiet, yielding strength.", "Watch a river carve a canyon. Water does not fight the rock; it flows around it, finds the low places and, over enough time, wears down stone. Few images capture Daoism better. The Daodejing, Daoism's most famous text, praises water again and again: it benefits all things without competing, settles in places others avoid and yet nothing can withstand it. Daoism, sometimes spelled Taoism, is one of China's great traditions, alongside Confucianism and Buddhism. It is both a philosophy about living in harmony with the natural flow of things and an organised religion with temples, priests and rituals. Many people in China, Taiwan and elsewhere draw on its ideas without calling themselves Daoists at all."],
    ["The Dao that cannot be named", "The Dao, or the Way, is the ultimate source and pattern of all things.", "Dao means 'way' or 'path'. In Daoism it refers to the ultimate reality behind everything: the source from which the universe arises and the natural pattern by which it unfolds. The Daodejing opens with a famous warning, usually translated along the lines of 'the Dao that can be spoken of is not the eternal Dao'. In other words, words and concepts can point towards it, but never fully capture it. The Dao is not a god who issues commands or judges people. It is more like the grain of the universe. The wise person, in this view, does not try to master or fight the world, but learns to recognise its patterns and move with them."],
    ["Laozi and the gatekeeper", "Laozi is the legendary author of the Daodejing, though historians doubt the story.", "Tradition credits the Daodejing to Laozi, whose name means 'Old Master'. A famous legend says he was a record keeper at the royal court of the Zhou dynasty. Weary of the decline of society, he rode west on a water buffalo to leave China. At the border pass, a gatekeeper recognised his wisdom and begged him to write down his teachings before departing. Laozi wrote a short book of about five thousand characters, handed it over and was never seen again. Most historians today think the Daodejing was compiled from earlier sayings, probably around the 300s or 400s BCE, and that Laozi may be partly or wholly legendary. The book is now one of the most translated texts in the world."],
    ["Wu wei: effortless action", "Wu wei means acting naturally and without strain, not doing nothing.", "One of Daoism's central ideas is wu wei, often translated as 'non action' but better understood as effortless or natural action. It does not mean being lazy. It means acting in harmony with circumstances rather than forcing them, like a skilled swimmer who uses the current instead of thrashing against it. A well known story from the Zhuangzi, another key Daoist text, describes a cook who carves an ox so skilfully that his knife never dulls, because he follows the natural spaces between the joints. Daoist writing applies the same idea to rulers: the best leaders, it suggests, govern so lightly that people barely notice them. Modern ideas about being in the 'flow' echo this ancient thought."],
    ["The butterfly dream", "Zhuangzi's playful writings question how sure we can be about reality.", "The second great Daoist thinker, Zhuangzi, lived around the 300s BCE and wrote with humour and imagination. In his most famous passage, he dreams he is a butterfly, fluttering happily with no idea he is Zhuangzi. Then he wakes and is unmistakably himself. But now he wonders: was he Zhuangzi dreaming of being a butterfly, or is he a butterfly now dreaming of being Zhuangzi? The story is less a riddle to solve than an invitation to hold our fixed ideas lightly. Zhuangzi loved stories that turn conventional values upside down: useless trees that survive because no carpenter wants them, and outcasts who turn out to be wiser than kings. His book remains one of the most enjoyable works of ancient philosophy."],
    ["Yin, yang and the immortals", "Religious Daoism includes temples, gods, rituals and the search for long life.", "The black and white yin yang symbol, though older than Daoism, is closely linked to it. Yin, the dark, receptive side, and yang, the bright, active side, are not good and evil but complementary forces, each holding a seed of the other. Around the 100s CE, Daoism also developed as an organised religion, often traced to a movement called the Way of the Celestial Masters. Religious Daoism has priests, temples, scriptures, colourful festivals and a large family of deities and immortals, such as the popular Eight Immortals. Some Daoists sought long life or even immortality through meditation, breathing exercises, diet and, in earlier times, alchemy. Some alchemical elixirs contained toxic substances like mercury, with deadly results."],
    ["Daoism in everyday life", "Daoist ideas live on in tai chi, Chinese medicine and popular culture.", "You may already have met Daoism without knowing it. Tai chi, the slow, flowing exercise practised in parks around the world, including many Australian ones, draws on Daoist ideas about balance and the flow of qi, or life energy. Traditional Chinese medicine and feng shui share some of the same concepts. In China, people often blend Confucian values about family and duty, Buddhist ideas about suffering and Daoist practices without seeing any contradiction, a mix sometimes called the 'three teachings'. In the West, Daoist ideas have influenced writers, poets and even a popular book, The Tao of Pooh, which explains Daoism through Winnie the Pooh. The core message travels well: slow down, notice nature and stop forcing things."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Which natural image does the Daodejing often use for yielding strength?", ["Fire", "Mountains", "Water", "Lightning"], 2, "Water yields and flows yet wears down stone, a favourite Daoist image."],
    [1, "What does the word Dao mean?", ["Heaven", "Way or path", "Emperor", "Energy"], 1, "Dao means way or path, referring to the ultimate source and pattern of all things."],
    [3, "What is the best description of wu wei?", ["Refusing to work", "Strict obedience to rulers", "Acting naturally and without strain", "Fasting for long life"], 2, "Wu wei means effortless action in harmony with circumstances, not doing nothing."],
    [4, "What does Zhuangzi wonder after his butterfly dream?", ["Whether butterflies can think", "Whether he is a man who dreamt of being a butterfly or a butterfly dreaming of being a man", "Why he cannot fly", "Whether dreams predict the future"], 1, "The famous story questions how certain we can be about which state is real."],
    [5, "How do Daoists view yin and yang?", ["As good and evil at war", "As complementary forces, each containing the other", "As two rival gods", "As heaven and hell"], 1, "Yin and yang are complementary, not opposing moral forces."]
  ]);

WB.L("maui-polynesian-myths", "beliefs", "beginner", "Māui and the myths of Polynesia", "He fished up islands, slowed the sun and stole fire. Māui, the trickster demigod, is a hero across thousands of kilometres of Pacific Ocean.",
  [
    ["A hero of the whole Pacific", "Māui appears in stories from Aotearoa New Zealand to Hawaiʻi.", "Spread your fingers across a map of the Pacific: from Aotearoa New Zealand in the south west to Hawaiʻi in the north and Rapa Nui, or Easter Island, in the east, lies the vast triangle of Polynesia. Across this huge ocean, island peoples who share ancestral roots tell stories of the same mischievous hero: Māui. The details change from place to place, but he is almost always clever, cheeky, brave and a little reckless. He is a demigod, part divine and part human, and his adventures explain features of the world. In many versions he is the youngest of several brothers, often underestimated, which makes his triumphs all the sweeter. This lesson focuses mostly on Māori versions, with a few others for comparison."],
    ["Born and cast away", "In Māori tradition, the baby Māui was cast into the sea and raised by the gods.", "In a well known Māori telling, Māui was born early and his mother, Taranga, thinking he would not survive, wrapped him in her topknot of hair and cast him into the sea. Seaweed and jellyfish protected him, and he was rescued and raised by a sky ancestor, Rangi. As a boy he found his way back to his mother's house and announced himself to his surprised family. His full name in some versions is Māui Tikitiki a Taranga, a reference to that topknot. The story establishes a pattern: Māui begins as an outsider who has to prove himself. Many listeners have found that idea powerful, because it suggests that unlikely beginnings can lead to remarkable lives."],
    ["Fishing up a land", "Māori tradition says Māui hauled up the North Island like a giant fish.", "The most famous Māui story in Aotearoa tells how he hid in the bottom of his brothers' canoe when they went fishing. Far out to sea, he revealed himself and lowered a magical hook made from the jawbone of his grandmother, Murirangawhenua. He caught something enormous and hauled it up: a vast fish, which became the North Island. Its Māori name, Te Ika a Māui, means the fish of Māui. His brothers began hacking at the fish before it had settled, which is said to explain the North Island's mountains and valleys. The South Island is often known as Te Waka a Māui, his canoe, and Stewart Island as Te Punga a Māui, its anchor stone."],
    ["Slowing the sun", "Māui snares the sun so that days are long enough to live and work.", "In Māui's time, the stories say, the sun raced across the sky so quickly that people did not have enough daylight to fish, farm and cook. Māui decided to fix this. With his brothers he plaited strong ropes and travelled to the place where the sun rose. They set a noose and, when the sun climbed out, they snared it. Māui beat the struggling sun with his grandmother's jawbone until it agreed to travel more slowly. In Hawaiian tradition, a similar feat happens on the summit of Haleakalā, whose name is often translated as 'house of the sun', on the island of Maui, which bears his name. The story neatly explains why the days are as long as they are."],
    ["Stealing fire", "Māui tricks the fire goddess and fire ends up hidden in trees.", "Another Māori story tells how Māui, curious about where fire came from, put out all the fires in the village one night. He then went to the underworld to visit Mahuika, the fire goddess and his ancestor, who kept fire in her fingernails. She gave him one fingernail of flame, but he deliberately put it out and returned for another, again and again, until she had only one left. Furious, she threw it at him and set the land ablaze. Māui called on rain to quench the flames, and the last sparks escaped into certain trees, such as the kaikōmako. That is why, the story says, people can make fire by rubbing sticks of those woods together."],
    ["The death of a trickster", "Māui's attempt to win immortality for humans ends in his death.", "Māui's final adventure is his most daring. He wanted to defeat death itself, so that people could live forever. Death was personified by Hine nui te pō, the great woman of the night, a guardian of the underworld. Māui planned to crawl through her body while she slept, which would reverse the course of death. He took birds with him and warned them to keep quiet. But as he crawled, the sight was so comical that a little fantail, the pīwakawaka, burst into laughter. Hine nui te pō woke and crushed Māui, and so all people remain mortal. Like Gilgamesh, the hero fails to escape death, and the story asks listeners to accept mortality as part of life."],
    ["Stories still alive", "Polynesian myths remain living culture, from Matariki to debates about film.", "These stories are not museum pieces. Many Māori trace their whakapapa, or genealogy, back through gods and ancestors, so myths and family history are deeply connected. Māori creation traditions describe the sky father Ranginui and earth mother Papatūānuku, pushed apart by their son Tāne to let light into the world. Matariki, the Māori new year marked by the rising of the Pleiades star cluster, became a New Zealand public holiday in 2022. Māui reached a global audience in Disney's 2016 film Moana. Many people enjoyed it, while some Pacific voices criticised the way Māui was portrayed and argued that sacred figures should be depicted with care. That debate shows how much these stories still matter to the people who own them."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Where are Māui stories told?", ["Only in Aotearoa New Zealand", "Across much of Polynesia, including Hawaiʻi", "Only in Australia", "Mainly in Japan"], 1, "Māui is a hero across Polynesia, with versions from Aotearoa to Hawaiʻi."],
    [2, "What does Te Ika a Māui, a name for the North Island, mean?", ["The canoe of Māui", "The anchor of Māui", "The fish of Māui", "The sun of Māui"], 2, "Te Ika a Māui means the fish of Māui, recalling the story of him hauling up the land."],
    [3, "Why did Māui snare the sun?", ["To steal its fire", "To make it travel more slowly so days were longer", "To make it stop forever", "To punish his brothers"], 1, "The sun raced across the sky, so Māui slowed it to give people enough daylight."],
    [5, "Which bird's laughter woke Hine nui te pō?", ["The kiwi", "The tūī", "The kererū", "The fantail, or pīwakawaka"], 3, "The fantail laughed at the sight of Māui crawling, waking the guardian of death."],
    [6, "What is Matariki?", ["A Māori war dance", "The Māori new year, marked by the rising of the Pleiades", "Māui's magic hook", "A Hawaiian volcano"], 1, "Matariki marks the Māori new year and became a New Zealand public holiday in 2022."]
  ]);
